WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.061 --> 00:00:03.330
- [Narrator] Mexican
chilies used in Sriracha,

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:05.280
Italian Risotto rice,

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.160
American corn that's used
in just about everything,

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.200
these staple crops are all vulnerable

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.240
to hot weather and a lack of water.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.680
And as many countries have been hit

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.420
by extreme droughts this year,

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:17.160
farmers are feeling the heat as yields

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.260
are a fraction of normal levels.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.030
Italian rice farmers for example,

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:23.010
are expected to lose 30 to 50%

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:26.070
of their yields this year
as paddy fields dry up.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:27.780
- When you have this hot weather

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.610
and drought this late of the year,

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:31.800
it really puts a lot of
stress on those plants

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.690
at a really key time in their growth.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:36.450
And that's why some economists think

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.640
that their crop yield could be stressed,

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.770
and the yields could go down.

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:42.270
- [Narrator] And commodities analysts say

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:45.300
that those lower yields
could increase prices.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:46.380
The string of droughts,

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.360
coupled with food and energy shortages,

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:49.890
and supply chain disruptions,

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:53.670
are expected to keep the cost
of staples high this winter.

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:55.260
Waterways that typically feed

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.210
the agricultural systems across the world

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.370
have been parched by drought this summer.

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:01.560
Italy's longest river, the river Po,

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.000
accounts for up to 40%

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.000
of the country's agricultural
production by market value.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.160
But water levels have
dropped well below normal

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.680
in recent months.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:13.180
(speaks foreign language)

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.030
In France, the country has faced

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.370
the most severe drought ever recorded.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:22.740
And during China's driest
summer in six decades,

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.350
water levels plummeted in
parts of the Yangtze river,

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:27.393
a crucial life source for crops.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.870
In the US, nearly 30% of corn production

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:32.430
is based in drought hit areas

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:33.810
as record high temperatures

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:36.090
have stifled parts of North America.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:36.923
- It's been hot,

00:01:36.923 --> 00:01:38.580
and hotter than normal.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:40.770
And I think one of the key things

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:42.870
to remember about the heat,

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:45.090
while it's been bad in a lot of places,

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:47.100
it's exacerbating some drought

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:49.200
that was in existing parts of the US.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.870
When you put that hot weather
on top of the drought,

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:54.420
that's what's really the key thing here.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:56.280
It's the drought and the heat wave

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:59.010
that has just been so bad this summer.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.200
And it's put a lot of stress on farmers.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:02.310
- Our yields are off.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:04.200
Our yields are probably off 25%.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.580
- A lot of ranchers are havin'

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:08.700
to reduce or liquidate their herds.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:13.700
- All these things compounding
could just be a decision

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.020
for a farmer to have to call it quits.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:19.320
- Drought affect prices fast,

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:20.910
let's say, at local level.

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:22.410
So let's say, for tomatoes

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:25.350
other producer here in Italy,

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:27.300
so they will be more expensive.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.700
If they have a,

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.100
let's say a reduction in the production,

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.070
but then in Europe at also global level,

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:37.050
we have this interconnection
in the markets.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:38.640
And for this, we look mainly

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:40.620
to the bread bag, baskets

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.530
that are the countries
that produce most grain.

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:48.530
South America, central US,
Ukraine, India, also China.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:51.300
- [Narrator] When a drought hits one

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.310
or more of these so-called bread baskets,

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:55.080
there's less supply of staple crops

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:56.250
in the global market.

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:58.770
And as a result, prices get pushed up.

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:02.040
This has happened before
during drought years like 2012,

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:03.660
and while prices are currently lower

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.910
than they were during March, 2022,

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:07.350
they are still historically high,

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:09.060
and are projected to go up even further

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:10.500
in the months ahead.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.150
Wheat prices are expected to jump

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:13.800
by 20 to 30% this year,

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.290
according to research by JP Morgan.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:18.720
Those changes in costs felt by customers

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.220
are being driven not only by drought,

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:21.660
but also by the war in Ukraine,

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:24.780
and continued pandemic related
supply chain disruptions.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:26.820
But analysts say the
changing weather patterns

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:27.660
we're seeing this year,

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:30.390
will continue to influence
key agricultural markets

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:31.860
well beyond this summer.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:33.060
Scientists have forecast

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:34.710
there will be an uptick in frequency,

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:37.950
and severity of droughts as
temperatures continue to rise.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:39.330
And that means, economies are going

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:40.620
to have to brace themselves

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:43.135
as droughts can threaten
global food security.

00:03:43.135 --> 00:03:45.552
(wind blows)

